WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.799
If you have ever stumbled onto one of those
click bait list articles with titles, like

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:11.230
world's top 10 smartest people, then you might
have seen this name come up.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:12.890
William James Sidis.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:19.859
His name is often included in these lists
alongside a claim that his IQ is between 250

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:20.859
and 300.

00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:28.210
For context that is higher than any recorded
result and anything above 140 is considered

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:29.689
genius.

00:00:29.689 --> 00:00:35.250
People like Steven Hawking and Einstein are
said to have IQs around 160.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:41.940
Now, from what I can tell this claim about
William Sidis' IQ seems highly dubious.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.710
In fact, I can't find any evidence that he
ever took an IQ test.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:54.740
It was his sister who said that a psychologist
had once measured his IQ to be between 250

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:55.740
and 300.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:02.550
But that might have come about from a misunderstanding
or an exaggeration when William did write

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:11.130
to his sister saying that he had just taken
a civil service exam for which he scored 254th

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:12.780
on the list of candidates.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.329
But he himself described that as not so encouraging.

00:01:17.329 --> 00:01:23.679
What is true about William Sidis is that he
was very intelligent and he was a child prodigy.

00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:29.829
The New York Times was reporting about him
when he was 10 years old and had just passed

00:01:29.829 --> 00:01:31.950
the MIT entrance exam.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.170
It says that when he was two, he could read
and write.

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.700
And when he was four, he could speak four
languages.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:45.110
What is also true about William Sidis is that
he has a very interesting story.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:50.060
And I'll share that story in this video, as
well as share some of the work that he left

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:51.060
behind.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.749
He set the record for the youngest person
to enroll at Harvard at age 11.

00:01:55.749 --> 00:02:00.380
And at age 12, he gave a lecture there on
four dimensional bodies.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:06.009
William's father, Boris Sidis was a psychiatrist
and his mother, Sarah was a doctor.

00:02:06.009 --> 00:02:12.319
They had a lot of thoughts about developmental
psychology and put a lot of effort into raising

00:02:12.319 --> 00:02:14.580
William to be a prodigy.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:21.989
They spent any spare money on books and learning
tools for him and nurtured his hunger for

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:22.989
knowledge.

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:30.000
William Sidis was named after his dad's friend,
William James, who was a philosopher and psychologist.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.780
It was Boris Sidis and William James, who
together came up with the idea that we only

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.830
use 10% of our brains.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.770
And they tested this theory on young William.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:48.110
When William showed great talents in learning,
it was seen as proof that most people only

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:52.060
meet a fraction of their full mental potential.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:58.569
It was called the reserve energy theory and
Boris was trying to unlock his son's reserve

00:02:58.569 --> 00:02:59.780
energy.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:06.890
This 10% of the brain idea is largely considered
a myth now with Wikipedia saying that it rests

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:13.159
in folklore, not science, and that brain mapping
suggests that all areas of the brain have

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:19.230
a function and that they are used nearly all
of the time, but keep that reserve energy

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:24.000
theory in your mind, because it's important
to this story in unexpected ways.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.830
Life as a child, prodigy, wasn't easy for
William.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:33.379
He was constantly reported on and struggled
to fit in with older students, both at school

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:35.640
and university.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:41.209
After he graduated his degree, he told reporters
that he wanted to live the perfect life, which

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:44.079
to him meant living in seclusion.

00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:49.790
He abandoned further studies in maths and
law, and even got arrested for participating

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:51.519
in a political parade.

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:57.849
After that incident, his parents tried to
reform him, but he soon became estranged from

00:03:57.849 --> 00:04:03.110
them and went on to live a very independent
and private life.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:08.359
He ended up suing The New Yorker for an article
they published about him calling him lonely

00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:10.829
and invading his privacy.

00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:17.440
The sentiment of that article as well as many
modern footnotes about him seemed to imply

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:24.620
that despite his great early promise, he amounted
to nothing or that he suffered a complete

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:30.110
mental breakdown due to being pushed too hard,
mentally as a child by his parents.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:36.789
But in all those years of William Sidis living
outside of the public eye, he was still learning

00:04:36.789 --> 00:04:39.660
and writing and sharing knowledge.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:44.500
He published many books under pseudonyms,
but I have one here that he published under

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:45.680
his own name.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:53.610
It is called The Animate and The Inanimate,
and it was published in 1920 when he was 22.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:59.600
It details a theory of his that relates to
cosmology, physics and life.

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.110
So let's have a look through it.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:08.210
Sidis says that this work is based on the
idea of the reversibility of everything in

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:09.210
time.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:15.430
That is that all of the physical laws work
backwards, but there is one exception and

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:18.949
that is the second law of thermodynamics.

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:24.819
The second law relates to the idea that say,
you have a bunch of particles in a box.

00:05:24.819 --> 00:05:32.300
They might start off all concentrated in one
corner, but over time they will spread out.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:38.630
And it's so extremely unlikely that they would
all collect in that corner again, that we

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:47.020
consider it to be impossible . With this we
see that the flow of heat goes from hot to

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:48.020
cold.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:54.160
You don't see water spontaneously turning
into ice because this is such an unlikely

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.889
flow of energy.

00:05:55.889 --> 00:06:01.919
Then Sidis mentions the idea of Maxwell's
demon, that if you were to have a reversal

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:08.370
of this second law of thermodynamics, that
you could imagine a sorting demon who could

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:15.009
separate the slower particles from the faster
ones, a demon could do this by monitoring

00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:21.069
some kind of barrier between two halves of
the box, only letting slow particles into

00:06:21.069 --> 00:06:25.340
the left hand side and fast particles into
the right.

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:31.240
You'd see that left hand side of the box become
cold and the right hand side become hot.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:35.990
Something that wouldn't naturally happen,
but seems to have made available some kind

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:39.009
of energy flow between hot and cold.

00:06:39.009 --> 00:06:45.949
The second law of thermodynamics also brought
in the idea of entropy, which is constantly

00:06:45.949 --> 00:06:52.389
increasing and for the next hundred or so
pages William Sidis explores the idea that

00:06:52.389 --> 00:06:59.520
perhaps in some regions of space, we do see
a reversal of the second law of thermodynamics.

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.479
And in these regions, entropy flows in the
other direction.

00:07:02.479 --> 00:07:09.509
He also claims that perhaps these regions
are what we know as life, that instead of

00:07:09.509 --> 00:07:16.280
defining life as something that is able to
reproduce or grow or respond to the environment

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:22.159
that we instead define it as something that
is able to take what we might have thought

00:07:22.159 --> 00:07:29.919
of as unavailable energy and turn it into
a reserve fund of energy used only by life

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:33.440
and created by non-living forces.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:38.830
And then he mentions the work of his father's
friend, professor William James, who discovered

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:42.990
in the domain of mental phenomena, what he
calls reserve energy.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:46.180
That's that whole 10% of the brain idea.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:51.610
So William Sidis is taking this idea that
he has been raised to believe in and that

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:54.280
his father was heavily involved with.

00:07:54.280 --> 00:08:00.009
And perhaps he credits for the reason he is
called the prodigy and tries to apply it to

00:08:00.009 --> 00:08:04.409
physics, to thermodynamics and the laws of
the universe.

00:08:04.409 --> 00:08:10.680
He does mention that this work is purely speculative
and that there are no experiments to verify

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:11.900
what he suggests.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:15.039
This work never really reached an audience.

00:08:15.039 --> 00:08:21.210
And perhaps that made Sidis feel despondent
about it, and he didn't publish any more known

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:22.710
works of physics.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:28.669
This work was pretty much unknown until it
was rediscovered in an attic in 1979.

00:08:28.669 --> 00:08:32.610
Now let's take a little look through some
more of the chapters.

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:37.329
The first few chapters look at some of the
physical laws and explore what they might

00:08:37.329 --> 00:08:39.070
look like in a reverse universe.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:46.120
He says that in the real universe, energy
runs down to a common level and in the reverse

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:50.790
universe, energy tends to build itself up
into different levels.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:57.079
In the real universe, we would see an ultimate
heat death mentioned here as when all of the

00:08:57.079 --> 00:09:03.290
energy is in the form of heat it's least concentrated
form and at a uniform concentration.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:06.470
A dead level of energy would be reached.

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:10.940
And after that, nothing further could ever
happen in the universe.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:17.459
That's unless heat was able to build itself
up into different levels and be converted

00:09:17.459 --> 00:09:22.769
back into mechanical or kinetic energy as
in a reverse universe.

00:09:22.769 --> 00:09:28.010
Now, I don't mean to make any of this sound
too believable, and there are many dubious

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:34.060
claims in here we've got here the requirement
that the mechanical efficiency in the reverse

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:39.900
universe is over a hundred percent, just like
how the second law of thermodynamics is really

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:43.670
an overwhelming probability rather than a
law.

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:49.860
So too is its reversal that in this reverse
universe, these probabilities are weighted

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:51.750
in the opposite direction.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:57.470
Sidis believes that tracing back, there must
be a time in the past when the available energy

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:02.850
was a hundred percent of the total universe,
and there must have been what he called a

00:10:02.850 --> 00:10:07.200
great collision, where everything was at a
temperature of absolute zero.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:13.170
And there were two semi universes, which were
moving towards each other in each of which

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:16.490
there was not even a trace of relative motion.

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:24.260
Sidis believes that in our own universe, 50%
of cases will obey the second law of thermodynamics.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:27.550
And in 50% of the cases, it will be reversed.

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:30.310
The universe as a whole will be neutral.

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:34.800
So where apparently is all of this reverse
universe, hiding?

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:41.209
Well Sidis believed that there were vast pockets
of space that have a tendency towards the

00:10:41.209 --> 00:10:46.660
positive direction and other pockets that
have a tendency in the reverse direction.

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:52.760
That the universe is made up of a number of
what we may call bricks, ultimately positive

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:58.850
and negative, all approximately the same volume
arranged sort of like a three dimensional

00:10:58.850 --> 00:10:59.850
checkerboard.

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:04.620
That what we see is simply the white space
that we are in.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:07.750
The black spaces surrounding us are invisible.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:13.020
In them the laws of thermodynamics would be
predominantly reversed.

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:20.390
The stars in those black spaces would be reverse
stars that instead of giving out light, they

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:21.450
absorb light.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:26.680
This is an intriguing part of his discussion
because he's doing a good job of describing

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:30.010
what we would now call black holes.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:34.430
He calls these reverse stars, perfectly black
bodies.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:39.160
These stars use radiant energy to build up
a higher level of heat.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:44.940
He says that we can only see stars in the
section of space where the second law of thermodynamics

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:50.600
prevails, but that there is an additional
part of the universe, which is not visible

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:51.790
to us.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:56.579
These black spaces even absorb the light from
the white spaces beyond them.

00:11:56.579 --> 00:11:59.589
So even those cannot be seen by us.

00:11:59.589 --> 00:12:05.670
And by looking at the distribution of light
in the sky, we get an idea merely of the size

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:08.930
and shape of our special white space.

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:13.310
And if you think that Sidis is predicting
the existence of black holes here, keep in

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:20.520
mind, this is 47 years before the term black
hole was even first used and almost 20 years

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:26.560
before Chandrasekhar even theorized the existence
of something like a black hole.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:31.900
He says the boundary surface is between the
positive and negative sections of space that

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:38.149
when light crosses it, it crosses in one direction
from the positive side to the negative side.

00:12:38.149 --> 00:12:43.590
If we were on the positive side, as seems
to be the case, then we could not see beyond

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:46.040
such surface though.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:52.720
We might easily have gravitational or other
evidence of bodies existing beyond the surface.

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:57.900
And that seems to have a lot in common with
what we know as the event horizon.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:05.630
Sidis then mentions a supernova and suggest
that the flaring up of a star must be it changing

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:12.050
over from the negative to the positive tendency
that in the universe stars will be constantly

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:18.029
crossing over from one section to the next
and that when this happens and a star crosses

00:13:18.029 --> 00:13:24.519
over from a positive to a negative section
of the universe, there happens a slow process

00:13:24.519 --> 00:13:30.300
of development of life growth, changing the
star from a positive one to a negative one

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:31.300
gradually.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:37.160
And here you can start to see where the title
of this book comes from The Animate and The

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:38.160
Inanimate.

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:45.050
Sidis says that inanimate phenomena are positive
tendencies and follow the second law of thermodynamics

00:13:45.050 --> 00:13:52.000
while animate phenomena on the contrary are
negative tendencies and tend to reverse that

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:53.000
law.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:57.920
There's a motivation behind a lot of these
stranger connections, which comes from the

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:04.009
fact that at the time this book was written
around 1920, there was a lot about biology

00:14:04.009 --> 00:14:07.190
and living processes that we didn't understand.

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:14.210
And we have learned so much more since then,
the way that biological processes work within

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:22.610
living bodies and the way that cells process
energy is much less of a mystery to us now.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:28.240
And a lot of what we know about biology wouldn't
stack up against some of these claims, even

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:31.430
the way our own brains work was a lot less
understood.

00:14:31.430 --> 00:14:35.990
Sidis calls it a central organ in which the
reserve energy is stored.

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:41.920
And it seems that if he had been raised in
any other family, he probably wouldn't have

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:46.399
had that idea and been trying to draw that
kind of a connection.

00:14:46.399 --> 00:14:51.930
Some of the claims get more and more dubious
as the pages go on, but Sidis himself even

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:56.540
recognizes this and ends with some objections
to his theory.

00:14:56.540 --> 00:15:01.020
He emphasizes that this work is only mere
speculation.

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:06.009
One objection is that we have no proof that
life really does reverse the second law of

00:15:06.009 --> 00:15:07.269
thermodynamics.

00:15:07.269 --> 00:15:11.880
Instead, we have plenty of evidence of living
bodies obeying the second law.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:17.770
The theory also supposes that life exists
everywhere and under all sorts of circumstances,

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:22.180
but that's in contrast to what we actually
observe when it comes to life.

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:27.839
It instead seems to be an extremely complex
phenomenon that can exist only under very

00:15:27.839 --> 00:15:29.900
special circumstances.

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:34.560
Sidis says that he will leave the reader to
compare for themself the theory, and weigh

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:37.560
up the various arguments for and against.

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:40.980
This book remained pretty much unknown for
many decades.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:47.290
When it was found again in 1979, it was given
to Buckminster Fuller who happened to be a

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:50.320
former Harvard classmate of William Sidis.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:54.620
Buckminster Fuller gave the work, perhaps
its first proper review.

00:15:54.620 --> 00:16:00.410
He said that he felt excitement and joy on
being handed a copy of the book, which he

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:02.800
says clearly predicts the black hole.

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:08.160
So many people commenting on Sidis' life have
said that he amounted to nothing and didn't

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:10.000
use any of his academic brilliance.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:16.690
But Fuller says here, I hope you'll become
as excited as I am at this discovery that

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:21.860
Sidis did go on after college to do the most
magnificent thinking and writing.

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:28.250
Sidis' work is hard to understand, and we
may never find a place for it within modern

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:33.620
theories, but Sidis himself was also hard
for society to understand.

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:39.590
People poked at his first signs of weakness,
perhaps to feel better about themselves.

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:45.040
William didn't deserve to be lumped with the
pressures of having to continuously prove

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:46.040
his genius.

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:51.899
That a child prodigy would grow up and collapse
under the mental burden of their childhood

00:16:51.899 --> 00:16:58.980
is an easier narrative to report on than understanding
the alternative ways that we all try to live

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:00.870
a fulfilling life.

